-- a line's key packed eight bytes to a word (Avik's one-source check held every long line of every prose
-- document as a list of bytes, a cell a byte, and outgrew a 7 GB machine at 19,624 index entries,
-- 2026-11-18): a key is compared and sorted as a list of words, an eighth as many cells. A line holds no
-- NUL byte, so packing with zero padding is injective: two lines are equal exactly when their packed keys
-- are, and the check stays exact. The last word's free low bytes are zero.
os-pad : Nat → Word → Word
os-pad zero a = a
os-pad (suc r) a = os-pad r (wmul a #256)
